// `run_scale_test` MCP tool. Wraps `maxon-shv2 scale-test` — the scaling INSTRUMENT — and returns
// its measurements as a STRUCTURED document.
//
// ⚠ IT HAS NO VERDICT, AND THIS TOOL MUST NOT INVENT ONE. scale-test reports numbers and their
// deltas from the last run recorded in `docs/optimization-log.md`; it renders no judgement about
// them, and there is nothing to pass. A `verdict` field here would put a green light in front of a
// caller who would then, reasonably, try to make it green — which is how the instrument came to be
// altered to stop it complaining, rather than the compiler being explained. `exitCode` is the only
// pass/fail thing in the envelope, and it means only ONE thing: non-zero iff the RUN BROKE and
// measured nothing (a rung would not compile, or the generated corpus folded away).
//
// ⚠ AND IT REPORTS NO WALL TIME. The document carries allocations, frees, bytes and CPU TICKS — per
// rung and per phase — and no milliseconds, no exponents, no residuals. Wall time counts every other
// process on the box, so it cannot be trended and is never collected; if a caller wants the
// milliseconds of one compile, that is the COMPILER's own instrument: `log: "compiler:debug"`.
// CPU ticks are different in kind: they count only the COMPILING THREAD, so preemption is invisible
// to them and they CAN be trended — and they are the only column that can see a cost which allocates
// nothing. They are not exact the way the memory columns are (a few percent of turbo/thermal/cache
// noise) and their unit is platform-defined, which is why `repeat` is in the document beside them and
// why a consumer compares RATIOS. The ladder DOUBLES, so the ratio between two rungs IS the growth
// (x2.00 linear, x4.00 quadratic) in every column, taken from the counts with a division.
//
// NO `compiler` ARGUMENT. Only shv2 has `scale-test`, so the tool is fixed to it, exactly as `fmt`
// is fixed to the C# bootstrap. This follows Compilers.maxon's stated discipline: never hand a
// compiler a flag it has never heard of. Offering a `compiler` field here would invite
// `compiler: "csharp"` and answer it with a usage dump.
//
// IT DOES NOT SCRAPE. The runner's stdout is a padded ASCII table built to be read by a person, and
// a scrape of it would silently mis-parse the day a phase name outgrew its column. So the tool asks
// the runner for `--result-json=<path>` and reads THAT — the same reason the compiler grew
// `--metrics=` rather than having the runner scrape its `--log=compiler:debug` table one level down.
// The returned doc carries the runner's own JSON verbatim, plus the envelope every tool here returns
// (command, exitCode, durationMs), plus the tail of the human report for a reader who wants it.
//
// Inputs (JSON):
//   rungs?: int          → --rungs=<n>          how many rungs to climb. Each DOUBLES the program.
//   repeat?: int         → --repeat=<n>         compiles per rung. CPU takes the MINIMUM of them;
//                                               memory takes the first and cross-checks the rest
//   note?: string        → --note=<text>        RECORD this run in docs/optimization-log.md, with
//                                               <text> as the reason the numbers moved
//   perType?: bool       → --per-type           also run the untimed --mm-trace pass (by type AND by scope)
//   emitCorpus?: string  → --emit-corpus=<dir>  dump the generated corpus and compile nothing
//   log?: string         → --log=<value>
//
// Output (JSON): { command, exitCode, durationMs, result: <the runner's document>, rawTail }

// A full six-rung ladder is ~20 s of compiling PER REPEAT — so ~60 s at the default `repeat` of 3 —
// and the top rungs are superlinear, so a slow or loaded machine can take several times that. Ten
// minutes is the same head room `run_spec_test` allows, and for the same reason: a runaway run should
// be reported, not silently killed.
//
// ⚠ THE TWO KNOBS MULTIPLY, and their product can exceed this: `rungs` is exponential and `repeat` is
// linear, so `rungs=8, repeat=25` is on the order of half an hour. That is not clamped here — a
// caller who asks for it gets this timeout, reported as an error with the elapsed time on it, which
// is the honest answer to a request for more compiling than ten minutes buys.
let defaultScaleTestTimeoutMs = 600000

// Where the runner is asked to leave its structured result. Under the corpus temp dir, which is
// already gitignored and already wiped per run — a result file is exactly as disposable as the
// corpus that produced it.
let scaleResultRelativePath = ".scale-tmp/scale-result.json"

// Enough of the human report to see the curve table and the delta from the last recorded run,
// without shipping the whole thing through the MCP envelope alongside the structured copy of the
// same numbers.
let scaleRawTailMaxBytes = 4096

/// Entry point called by Server.maxon's dispatch table. `repoRoot` is the tree whose
/// shv2 is measured, and — when `note` is given — whose `docs/optimization-log.md`
/// gains a row. Resolved and validated by the dispatcher.
export function scaleTestHandler(argsDoc JsonDoc, argsId JsonNodeId, repoRoot FilePath) returns ToolOutcome
	let binary = try compilerBinary(repoRoot, compiler: Compiler.shv2) otherwise 'noBin'
		return ToolOutcome.error(RpcStandardErrorCode.invalidRequest.rawValue, message: "scale-test: the shv2 compiler has not been built. Build it first via the `build` tool (target: \"shv2\").")
	end 'noBin'

	let resultPath = childPath(repoRoot, segment: scaleResultRelativePath)

	// A STALE result file is worse than none. If the run breaks before writing one, a leftover from a
	// previous run would be read back and reported as THIS run's measurements — numbers from a run
	// that never happened. Delete it up front so its absence is meaningful.
	try File.delete(resultPath) otherwise ignore

	let argv = buildScaleTestArgv(argsDoc, argsId: argsId, resultPath: resultPath)
	let displayCommand = displayCommandFor(binary, argv: argv)

	let timing = Clock.nowMs()
	// `workingDirectory: repoRoot` is load-bearing, not tidiness. The runner resolves the corpus, the
	// trend log, and the rung paths it hands the compiler ALL relative to the working directory — and
	// the rung paths in particular must stay relative, because an absolute path is a String the
	// compiler allocates and its LENGTH would shift the byte column of the trend log, which is a fact
	// about the compiler and must not become a fact about where the repo lives (see ScaleTestRunner's
	// CorpusRelativeRoot).
	let result = try Subprocess.run(Executable.path(binary), arguments: argv, workingDirectory: repoRoot, timeoutMs: defaultScaleTestTimeoutMs) otherwise (err) 'spawn'
		return ToolOutcome.error(RpcStandardErrorCode.internalError.rawValue, message: "scale-test: {err.displayReason()} (after {Clock.elapsedMs(timing)}ms)")
	end 'spawn'
	let elapsed = Clock.elapsedMs(timing)

	return buildScaleOutcome(displayCommand, result: result, durationMs: elapsed, resultPath: resultPath)
end 'scaleTestHandler'

// =============================================================================
// Argument parsing
// =============================================================================

function buildScaleTestArgv(argsDoc JsonDoc, argsId JsonNodeId, resultPath FilePath) returns StringArray
	var argv = StringArray.create()
	argv.push("scale-test")
	argv.push("--result-json={resultPath.path}")

	let rungs = try argsDoc.getInt(argsId, key: "rungs") otherwise 0
	if rungs > 0 'haveRungs'
		argv.push("--rungs={rungs}")
	end 'haveRungs'

	// Forwarded verbatim, INCLUDING a value the runner will reject. `--repeat` is refused and never
	// clamped there, and clamping it here would put this tool's opinion of a valid repeat count beside
	// the runner's — two copies of one rule, which is the bug this repo keeps paying for. A caller who
	// asks for something impossible gets the runner's own refusal and a non-zero exitCode.
	let repeat = try argsDoc.getInt(argsId, key: "repeat") otherwise 0
	if repeat > 0 'haveRepeat'
		argv.push("--repeat={repeat}")
	end 'haveRepeat'

	let note = try argsDoc.getString(argsId, key: "note") otherwise ""
	if not note.isEmpty() 'haveNote'
		argv.push("--note={note}")
	end 'haveNote'

	let perType = try argsDoc.getBool(argsId, key: "perType") otherwise false
	if perType 'havePerType'
		argv.push("--per-type")
	end 'havePerType'

	let emitCorpus = try argsDoc.getString(argsId, key: "emitCorpus") otherwise ""
	if not emitCorpus.isEmpty() 'haveEmitCorpus'
		argv.push("--emit-corpus={emitCorpus}")
	end 'haveEmitCorpus'

	let log = try argsDoc.getString(argsId, key: "log") otherwise ""
	if not log.isEmpty() 'haveLog'
		argv.push("--log={log}")
	end 'haveLog'

	return argv
end 'buildScaleTestArgv'

// =============================================================================
// Outcome construction
// =============================================================================

function buildScaleOutcome(command String, result CollectedOutput, durationMs DurationMs, resultPath FilePath) returns ToolOutcome
	let combined = combineStreams(result.stdout, stderr: result.stderr)

	var doc = JsonDoc.create()
	var keys = StringArray.create()
	var children = JsonNodeIdArray.create()

	keys.push("command")
	children.push(doc.add(JsonNode.stringNode(command)))
	keys.push("exitCode")
	children.push(doc.add(JsonNode.numberNode(result.exitCode() as JsonFloat)))
	keys.push("durationMs")
	children.push(doc.add(JsonNode.numberNode(durationMs as JsonFloat)))

	// The runner's own structured document, spliced in whole. When it is absent the run BROKE before
	// it could write one — a crash, a timeout, a rung that would not compile, a corpus that folded
	// away — and that is reported as an `error`, not as an empty result, because an empty result would
	// read as "nothing to report" when the truth is "nothing was measured".
	//
	// `error` is NOT a verdict about the compiler and must never be read as one. It says the RUN
	// failed. The measurements, when there are any, carry no verdict at all.
	let resultText = try File.readText(resultPath) otherwise 'noResult'
		return brokenRunOutcome(doc, keys: keys, children: children, message: "scale-test wrote no result document — it exited {result.exitCode()} before completing, so nothing was measured. The tail of its output is in `rawTail`.", combined: combined)
	end 'noResult'

	let parsed = try Json.parse(resultText) otherwise (err) 'badJson'
		return brokenRunOutcome(doc, keys: keys, children: children, message: "scale-test's result document is not valid JSON ({err.name}).", combined: combined)
	end 'badJson'

	keys.push("result")
	children.push(copyJsonNode(parsed, srcId: parsed.root, dst: doc))

	keys.push("rawTail")
	children.push(doc.add(JsonNode.stringNode(lastBytes(combined, max: scaleRawTailMaxBytes))))

	doc.root = doc.add(JsonNode.objectNode(keys, children: children))
	return ToolOutcome.success(doc, root: doc.root)
end 'buildScaleOutcome'

// A run that produced no measurements. The envelope (command, exitCode, durationMs) is already in
// `keys`/`children`; this finishes it with the reason and the tail of the output.
function brokenRunOutcome(doc JsonDoc, keys StringArray, children JsonNodeIdArray, message String, combined String) returns ToolOutcome
	keys.push("error")
	children.push(doc.add(JsonNode.stringNode(message)))
	keys.push("rawTail")
	children.push(doc.add(JsonNode.stringNode(lastBytes(combined, max: scaleRawTailMaxBytes))))
	doc.root = doc.add(JsonNode.objectNode(keys, children: children))
	return ToolOutcome.success(doc, root: doc.root)
end 'brokenRunOutcome'
